WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.407
(slow upbeat music)

00:00:02.407 --> 00:00:07.010
- [Brian] I cannot see, but I
can hear the world around me.

00:00:07.010 --> 00:00:08.540
- [Narrator] Meet Brian,

00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:12.553
he's known as the world's best
totally blind mountain biker.

00:00:12.553 --> 00:00:14.847
(bell dinging)

00:00:14.847 --> 00:00:18.955
- [Brian] When I was younger
I had functional vision,

00:00:18.955 --> 00:00:20.330
and I played sports.

00:00:20.330 --> 00:00:22.943
I had dreams of becoming
a professional athlete.

00:00:22.943 --> 00:00:25.440
- [Narrator] And then
suddenly when he was 14,

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:27.100
he completely lost his sight over

00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:28.800
the course of three months.

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:29.740
His optic nerves

00:00:29.740 --> 00:00:33.200
inexplicably failed, and all
he could see was darkness.

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:35.870
He wasn't able to bike ride
or participate in the sports

00:00:35.870 --> 00:00:37.140
he once enjoyed.

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:38.890
- [Brian] I couldn't play hockey;

00:00:38.890 --> 00:00:40.676
I had to stop roller blading,

00:00:40.676 --> 00:00:43.504
couldn't read the classroom board anymore.

00:00:43.504 --> 00:00:45.033
My world had shrunk.

00:00:45.033 --> 00:00:47.770
- [Narrator] He had to find a new way

00:00:47.770 --> 00:00:49.075
to navigate the world.

00:00:49.075 --> 00:00:52.170
He found his cane limiting,
but when he learned

00:00:52.170 --> 00:00:53.610
to make a tongue click,

00:00:53.610 --> 00:00:55.465
his world changed again for the better.

00:00:55.465 --> 00:00:58.623
And it wasn't long until
he ventured back outdoors.

00:00:59.596 --> 00:01:03.570
- [Brian] I really missed bike riding,

00:01:03.570 --> 00:01:05.370
and I really wanted to be active,

00:01:05.370 --> 00:01:07.470
I just didn't know how to do it.

00:01:07.470 --> 00:01:09.660
As a blind rider it was a goal for me

00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:11.280
to get off the tandem

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:13.640
and have the experience
of being able to pilot

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:15.070
my own bike.

00:01:15.070 --> 00:01:16.980
It all starts with a zip tie.

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:18.970
A sighted person would attach it to

00:01:18.970 --> 00:01:21.890
their frame and angle into the bike wheel.

00:01:21.890 --> 00:01:23.780
The bike wheel would make noise

00:01:23.780 --> 00:01:27.134
and the sound would follow
while we still actively click.

00:01:27.134 --> 00:01:28.470
- [Narrator] Eventually,

00:01:28.470 --> 00:01:30.350
Brian was able to mountian bike alone

00:01:30.350 --> 00:01:31.930
and without the zip tie.

00:01:31.930 --> 00:01:33.100
- [Brian] When I'm riding my bike

00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:34.330
down a mountain,

00:01:34.330 --> 00:01:37.472
it's the same thing bats
are using, eco-location.

00:01:37.472 --> 00:01:40.466
I can make my active
sonar signal by making

00:01:40.466 --> 00:01:41.683
a tongue click.

00:01:41.683 --> 00:01:43.622
I start off with trying to find

00:01:43.622 --> 00:01:47.120
the object furthest in the
distance and then piece

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:49.241
the world together with
things closer and closer.

00:01:49.241 --> 00:01:50.690
- [Narrator] Brian has to enter

00:01:50.690 --> 00:01:52.450
an almost meditative state

00:01:52.450 --> 00:01:54.293
and remain extremely focused.

00:01:54.293 --> 00:01:56.580
- [Brian] Everything has to leave my brain

00:01:56.580 --> 00:01:59.110
and those sounds are painting a picture

00:01:59.110 --> 00:02:02.983
in my head-a vivid world
of acoustic images.

00:02:03.893 --> 00:02:07.318
- [Narrator] He didn't keep his passion

00:02:07.318 --> 00:02:09.000
to himself though.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:11.200
He's helped teach over thousands of people

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:13.300
to hear their world a little bigger.

00:02:13.300 --> 00:02:14.980
- [Brian] This is a teachable thing.

00:02:14.980 --> 00:02:16.440
Anybody can do this.

00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:18.030
And I'm happy to show them.

00:02:18.030 --> 00:02:21.780
Riding my bike was something
I never thought I'd be able

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:23.991
to do again, and now that I'm doing it,

00:02:23.991 --> 00:02:26.024
it serves as a great example

00:02:26.024 --> 00:02:29.023
to challenge preconceived ideas.

